<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    References ONLY Agora.Core. This suite must pass on a machine with no copy of
    Cities: Skylines II installed — that is the check that keeps the Core/Mod split honest.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.11.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2" />

    <!--
      Newtonsoft, only so the linked LLM files below compile here. Agora.Mod itself references the
      copy that ships with the game (src/Agora.Mod/CLAUDE.md: do not add a JSON dependency) — this
      package reference is confined to the test project, which has no game to take one from, and
      never reaches the deployed mod.

      Pinned to the version the game ships (…\Cities2_Data\Managed\Newtonsoft.Json.dll is 13.0.2),
      not the newest patch. These tests exist to assert what the deployed mod does; a parser
      behaviour difference between two patch releases would let them assert something else while
      still passing, which is the one failure the link-by-compile arrangement is here to prevent.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" Version="13.0.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Agora.Core\Agora.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Twenty-four Agora.Mod files compiled in BY LINK, not by project reference.

    politicsmodplan.md §7 and tests/CLAUDE.md suite 4 put "every palette entry proves its caps" in
    this suite, and non-negotiable #8 puts the clock here too — but both of those layers live in
    Agora.Mod, which cannot be project-referenced: it needs the game installed and this suite must
    not.

    The response reader and the JSON parser it sits on are here for a different reason: extracting
    the flavor document out of whatever the CLI printed is pure string work with a history of
    off-by-one bugs (a slogan containing a brace or a backslash used to truncate the document), and
    it is exactly the kind of thing that must be covered by a suite that runs without launching the
    game. The prompt builder is the same argument from the other end — its cap logic silently
    decides what the model is allowed to read, and the sections it must never cut are the ones that
    fail invisibly when they are. FlavorRequest, FlavorCatalog, FlavorSchema and FlavorLog come with
    it because they are what it is written against; ColossalFlavorLog, the one game-facing type in
    Llm/, is deliberately in a file of its own so that it does not.

    FlavorCacheMigration is here for the sharpest reason of the lot. It is the only thing standing
    between a tightened article limit and the loss of every cached party name, because a schema error
    is fatal to the whole flavor document while a pruned article costs one story — and the difference
    between those two outcomes is invisible in the code and obvious in a fixture. It was written
    against System.* and Newtonsoft only, as a design constraint rather than a happy accident, so
    that it could be linked here.

    The validation chain — FlavorValidator, JsonSchemaSubsetValidator, NumericFieldScanner and the
    FlavorDocument they hand off to — is the strongest reason of all, and it was the last to be
    linked. The numeric sweep is the sole executable enforcement of non-negotiable #1: no number
    entering engine state may originate from LLM output. tests/CLAUDE.md suite 2 already asks for
    "a numeric field smuggled into politics_flavor.json fails the build", and that sentence is only
    true if the sweep is compiled here and exercised here. Leaving the rule to be enforced by an
    assembly the test suite cannot load is how #1 comes to be enforced by nobody.

    The canned pool — StaticPoolProvider and the word lists in StaticPoolContent — is here because a
    party's name is identity that persists for the life of a save, and the draw that produces it is
    the one place where a wrong seed component is invisible in a screenshot and obvious only over
    time. It was keyed on the request date once, which renamed every party every sim month; the
    regression guard for that is a test that generates twice at different dates and compares, and it
    can only exist if the provider compiles here.

    The sidecar — SidecarSchema and the five files it loads beside — is here because
    /schema-change step 5 says an untested migration is a guess, and until now no test project could
    see a line of the migration engine. Non-negotiable #6 is the reason it matters more than most:
    "load must never desync", which in practice means a migration must be idempotent and must not
    drop a field the running build has not heard of. Neither property is visible by reading the
    code; both are trivial to assert against a fixture at the old version. SidecarStore comes with
    SidecarSchema because the round trip a player actually performs — a v1 file on disk, loaded,
    migrated, materialised, saved, loaded again — runs through the store, and a step that is correct
    in isolation but wrong in that sequence is the failure the fixture-only test would miss.
    AgoraSidecarSystem, the one game-facing type in Persistence/, is deliberately in a file of its
    own so that it does not come along.

    All twenty-four files are deliberately free of every Game.*, Colossal.* and Unity.* type (they
    use only System.*, Newtonsoft and Agora.Core), so linking them keeps the property this suite
    exists to protect: it still builds and passes on a machine with no copy of Cities: Skylines II.
    Adding a file here that does reference the game will fail this project's build immediately, which
    is the guard — and if a game type is ever added to one of these twenty-four, that is the alarm
    we want, because it means logic the tests cover has drifted out of their reach.

    Everything is in THIS project rather than in a sibling test project so that the one gate command
    in CLAUDE.md — dotnet test tests\Agora.Core.Tests\Agora.Core.Tests.csproj — runs all of it. A
    second suite that the gate command does not run is a suite that rots.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Effects\ModifierDelta.cs" Link="ModEffects\ModifierDelta.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Effects\EffectLedger.cs" Link="ModEffects\EffectLedger.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Time\SimClockMath.cs" Link="ModTime\SimClockMath.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Time\StartYearDelivery.cs" Link="ModTime\StartYearDelivery.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Persistence\SidecarSchema.cs" Link="ModPersistence\SidecarSchema.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Persistence\AgoraJson.cs" Link="ModPersistence\AgoraJson.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Persistence\AtomicFile.cs" Link="ModPersistence\AtomicFile.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Persistence\SidecarPaths.cs" Link="ModPersistence\SidecarPaths.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Persistence\LoadReconciliation.cs" Link="ModPersistence\LoadReconciliation.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Persistence\SidecarStore.cs" Link="ModPersistence\SidecarStore.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Llm\FlavorJsonReader.cs" Link="ModLlm\FlavorJsonReader.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Llm\ClaudeResponseReader.cs" Link="ModLlm\ClaudeResponseReader.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Llm\FlavorLog.cs" Link="ModLlm\FlavorLog.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Llm\FlavorCatalog.cs" Link="ModLlm\FlavorCatalog.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Llm\FlavorSchema.cs" Link="ModLlm\FlavorSchema.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Llm\FlavorCacheMigration.cs" Link="ModLlm\FlavorCacheMigration.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Llm\FlavorRequest.cs" Link="ModLlm\FlavorRequest.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Llm\FlavorPromptBuilder.cs" Link="ModLlm\FlavorPromptBuilder.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Llm\FlavorDocument.cs" Link="ModLlm\FlavorDocument.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Llm\JsonSchemaSubsetValidator.cs" Link="ModLlm\JsonSchemaSubsetValidator.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Llm\NumericFieldScanner.cs" Link="ModLlm\NumericFieldScanner.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Llm\FlavorValidator.cs" Link="ModLlm\FlavorValidator.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Llm\StaticPoolContent.cs" Link="ModLlm\StaticPoolContent.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Agora.Mod\Llm\StaticPoolProvider.cs" Link="ModLlm\StaticPoolProvider.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
